<h1>Pre-Install Guard</h1>
<p class="subtitle">An explicit review point for a narrow class of public npm installs—not a transparent package-manager wrapper.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Experimental and npm-only.</strong> Guard requires a target project with <code>package.json</code> and <code>package-lock.json</code>. It refuses pnpm, Yarn, npx, workspaces, bare installs, local/git/URL/tarball specs, arbitrary npm flags, and commands outside its grammar.</div>

<h2>Usage</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">vexes guard -- npm install package-name</span></div>
<p>Accepted verbs are narrow explicit forms of <code>npm install</code>, <code>npm i</code>, and <code>npm add</code> with public-registry package coordinates and a small allowlist of supported flags.</p>

<h2>Assessment flow</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Copy the target manifest and lockfile into a disposable resolver project.</li>
  <li>Resolve the proposed lockfile with lifecycle scripts disabled and without mutating the target.</li>
  <li>Preserve and diff individual lockfile occurrences.</li>
  <li>Require each new/changed artifact to use a public npm HTTPS URL and carry an integrity value.</li>
  <li>Cross-check exact-version registry metadata and collect heuristic/advisory evidence for changed occurrences.</li>
  <li>In human mode, apply an approved graph with <code>npm install --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund</code>.</li>
  <li>Require the final manifest and lockfile occurrence/version/resolved/integrity fields to match the approval.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Decision behavior</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>Default</th><th><code>--force</code></th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Known OSV advisory</td><td>Block</td><td>Still block</td></tr>
    <tr><td>CRITICAL heuristic evidence</td><td>Block</td><td>Still block</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HIGH heuristic evidence</td><td>Prompt in a TTY; block non-interactively</td><td>May allow</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Incomplete requested evidence</td><td>Block with exit 2</td><td>Still block</td></tr>
    <tr><td>No blocking evidence in completed checks</td><td>Allow</td><td>Allow</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>JSON and setup modes</h2>
<p>JSON mode is assessment-only and does not install. <code>--setup</code> is disabled and fails closed because a general shell wrapper would intercept npm commands outside guard's supported grammar. <code>--uninstall</code> exists only to remove wrapper blocks written by older releases, with backup and malformed-marker refusal checks.</p>

<h2>What the approval binds—and what it does not</h2>
<p>Guard binds its decision to manifest and lockfile metadata. It does not independently hash the bytes ultimately written to <code>node_modules</code>. It is not transactional: reviewed manifest/lockfile files are replaced atomically per file and restored on detected failure or drift, but npm can leave <code>node_modules</code> partially changed. An actively racing process under the same user account remains outside this boundary. Lifecycle scripts remain disabled during the guarded install.</p>

<div class="info-box"><strong>Practical consequence:</strong> guard can add evidence and constrain one install path. It cannot certify a package, replace review/testing, or protect unguarded package-manager commands.</div>
